CAT = "ukfawRole"; SEG = "ROL"
Q = [
("Which regulations require employers in Great Britain to provide adequate first-aid equipment and people?",
 "The First-Aid Regulations 1981", ["The RIDDOR Regulations 2013", "The COSHH Regulations 2002", "The Working Time Regulations 1998"],
 "The Health and Safety (First-Aid) Regulations 1981 require employers to provide adequate and appropriate first-aid equipment, facilities and people. RIDDOR covers reporting incidents, not first-aid provision."),
("Under RIDDOR, which of these fractures to a worker is a specified injury that must be reported?",
 "A broken arm", ["A broken finger", "A broken thumb", "A broken toe"],
 "RIDDOR lists fractures as specified injuries, except fractures to fingers, thumbs and toes. A broken finger may still need reporting if it keeps the worker off normal duties for over seven days."),
("Which safety sign marks first-aid equipment and first-aid rooms at work?",
 "A white cross on green", ["A red cross on white", "A white cross on red", "A green cross on white"],
 "Under the Safety Signs and Signals Regulations 1996, first-aid signs show a white cross on a green background. Green is the colour of safe condition signs."),
("An adult casualty is unresponsive and cannot give consent. What should you do?",
 "Give the care they need", ["Wait for a relative to agree", "Wait for a manager to agree", "Leave them until they respond"],
 "Consent is implied when a casualty is unresponsive, so you can give the first aid they need. A conscious adult with capacity must agree before you treat them."),
("You arrive first at an incident in a warehouse. What should you do before anything else?",
 "Check for danger", ["Check for a response", "Open the airway", "Start chest compressions"],
 "Danger comes first in the primary survey. A first-aider who becomes a casualty cannot help anyone, so make the area safe for you, bystanders and the casualty before approaching."),
("In the primary survey, which check comes straight after opening the airway?",
 "Breathing", ["Circulation", "Response", "Danger"],
 "The order is Danger, Response, Airway, Breathing, then Circulation. Opening the airway first lets you judge whether the casualty is breathing normally."),
("What is the main purpose of the secondary survey?",
 "To find other injuries", ["To check the scene is safe", "To restart a stopped heart", "To decide who calls 999"],
 "The secondary survey is a head-to-toe check and history taken once life-threatening problems are dealt with. Scene safety belongs to the primary survey."),
("What does the AVPU scale measure?",
 "Level of response", ["Breathing rate", "Amount of blood lost", "Severity of pain"],
 "AVPU stands for Alert, Voice, Pain, Unresponsive. It gives a quick, repeatable measure of how responsive a casualty is, so changes can be passed on to the ambulance crew."),
("On the AVPU scale, what does the P mean?",
 "Responds to pain only", ["Pupils react to light", "Pulse can be felt", "Patient is breathing"],
 "P means the casualty responds only to a painful stimulus, not to your voice. It ranks just above U for unresponsive."),
("What is the best way to protect yourself from infection when treating a bleeding wound?",
 "Wear disposable gloves", ["Use a clean cotton wool pad", "Hold a tissue over it", "Wash hands only after"],
 "Disposable gloves form a barrier between you and the casualty's blood. You should still wash your hands before and after treatment, but washing alone does not stop contact during it."),
("How should blood-soaked dressings be disposed of at work?",
 "In a clinical waste bag", ["In a general waste bin", "In a mixed recycling bin", "Down the nearest toilet"],
 "Contaminated waste should be sealed in a clinical waste bag and disposed of under the workplace's arrangements. General and recycling bins put cleaners and others at risk."),
("HSE's suggested low-hazard first-aid kit contains how many pairs of disposable gloves?",
 "At least three pairs", ["At least one pair", "At least twenty pairs", "At least six pairs"],
 "HSE guidance on first-aid kits suggests at least three pairs of disposable gloves for a low-hazard workplace. There is no mandatory list; the needs assessment decides the contents."),
("Which item should not be kept in a workplace first-aid kit?",
 "Tablets or medicines", ["Triangular bandages", "Sterile wound dressings", "Disposable gloves"],
 "HSE says first-aid kits should not contain tablets or medicines. Triangular bandages, eye pads and safety pins are all on its suggested list."),
("Who decides how many first-aiders a workplace needs?",
 "The employer", ["The HSE inspector", "The local council", "The first-aiders"],
 "The employer decides, based on a first-aid needs assessment of hazards, staff numbers, shifts and location. HSE's numbers are suggestions only."),
("A low-hazard office has 20 staff. What is the minimum HSE suggests?",
 "An appointed person", ["One FAW first-aider", "Two EFAW first-aiders", "A first-aid room"],
 "For low-hazard workplaces with fewer than 25 people, HSE suggests at least one appointed person. From 25 to 50 it suggests at least one EFAW-trained first-aider."),
("In a low-hazard workplace of over 50 people, how many FAW first-aiders does HSE suggest?",
 "One for every 100 people", ["One for every 25 people", "One for every 50 people", "One for every 200 people"],
 "HSE suggests at least one FAW-trained first-aider for every 100 people in a low-hazard workplace. One for every 50 is its suggestion for higher-hazard workplaces."),
("In a higher-hazard workplace of over 50 people, how many FAW first-aiders does HSE suggest?",
 "One for every 50 people", ["One for every 100 people", "One for every 25 people", "One for every 200 people"],
 "For higher-hazard work such as construction or manufacturing with more than 50 people, HSE suggests at least one FAW first-aider for every 50 employed."),
("What is the role of an appointed person?",
 "Take charge and call 999", ["Give FAW-level treatment", "Train the first-aiders", "Investigate every accident"],
 "An appointed person takes charge of first-aid arrangements, looks after the kit and calls the emergency services. They do not need first-aid training and should not give treatment they are not trained for."),
("Under RIDDOR, within how many days must an over-seven-day injury be reported?",
 "15 days", ["7 days", "10 days", "30 days"],
 "Injuries that keep a worker off normal duties for more than seven consecutive days must be reported within 15 days of the accident. Deaths and specified injuries are reported without delay."),
("Under RIDDOR, who has the duty to report a reportable workplace injury?",
 "The employer", ["The first-aider", "The injured worker", "The casualty's GP"],
 "RIDDOR puts the duty on the responsible person, normally the employer or the person in control of the premises. The first-aider's job is to record what happened and what they did."),
("For at least how long must records of reportable incidents be kept under RIDDOR?",
 "3 years", ["6 months", "1 year", "10 years"],
 "RIDDOR requires records of reportable incidents to be kept for at least three years. An accident book is a common way to keep them."),
("A conscious, alert adult refuses your help after a fall. What should you do?",
 "Respect it and monitor them", ["Treat them regardless of it", "Leave them straight away", "Wait for a manager's say"],
 "A conscious adult with capacity can refuse treatment. Stay nearby if you can, keep watching for changes, call 999 if their condition worsens and record the refusal."),
("Which organisation publishes the resuscitation guidelines that UK first aid courses follow?",
 "Resuscitation Council UK", ["Health and Safety Executive", "Care Quality Commission", "Royal College of Nursing"],
 "Resuscitation Council UK publishes the UK's CPR and first aid guidelines, most recently in October 2025. HSE sets what a workplace first aid course must cover, not the clinical steps."),
("What should a first-aid record include?",
 "Date, time and treatment given", ["The casualty's salary", "Their home address only", "Their National Insurance number"],
 "A useful record states the date, time and place, the casualty's name, what happened, the injury and the treatment given, and what happened next. Pay and NI numbers are not needed."),
]
